/*****************************************************************************
    Plan, smallest first
*****************************************************************************/
THE PRE-PASS DESIGN (Clay, 2026-11-12; forwarded by Tony; no ruling needed to start T1). Style and run resolution move from the C++ floor into kant; the floor keeps measuring, line breaking and painting until the jitter can take more.
    kant pre-pass   splits a field's text into paragraphs at blank lines; parses each into runs ({style: text}, nesting, {{); resolves insertions; resolves each run's style from the field's text style, pushing and popping run styles; warns by name on an unknown style and prints it plain.
    C++ floor       measures text in a font, breaks lines to the frame width (two-line rule later), clips overflow on a space, paints each line. It never looks up a style.
    hand-over       ONE floor call per field, drawSpans(field, spans), one request group. The span list is paragraphs, each a list of spans; a span is text plus its resolved font, colour and italic. It is dropped after drawing.
    layout context  fresh for each pass, in kant: the style stack, the current paragraph, the warnings.
    T1  the kant pre-pass builds the span list and the floor's run parsing gives way to drawSpans. Test: firstLight's PDF matches the C++ slice's by value, every typesetting row green.
    T2  retire the C++ run-parsing and style-lookup code (same session if small).      T3  insertions.      Later, after the jitter: line breaking and the two-line rule move to kant.
    T1'S OPENER, measured at seal 469 (a probe, not built): kant has no accessor that takes a string apart by position, so the pre-pass is a small kant grammar driven by tell. Four rules --
        tsPlain isRule plain^=[^{}]+;   tsRun isRule "{"- style=[a-zA-Z0-9]+ ":"- body^=[^{}]+ "}"-;   tsPiece isRule tsRun; tsPlain;   tsPara isRule tsPiece+;
        -- matched all 39 characters of "one two {emphasis: three four} five six" and returned tsPara holding three pieces (plain, run, plain). NOT yet measured: whether the spaces at a piece's edges survive (open question 4), nesting, {{, and the blank-line split.
